    intro: 'Chairs hang on chains from the arms of a spinning ride. As the ride speeds up, the chairs swing **outwards** until the chain’s tension — tilted at θ — has a vertical part that balances the weight and a horizontal part that supplies the centripetal force.\n\nIt is a conical pendulum whose pivot is itself carried round a circle of radius d.',
    predict: { q: 'Two riders, one twice as heavy, sit in chairs on equal chains. At steady speed, the heavier rider’s chain makes an angle that is…', options: ['larger', 'smaller', 'the same', 'zero'] },
    explore: '- Speed the ride up and slow it down — the chairs swing out and settle.\n- Set the arm length d to 0: a conical pendulum (with its threshold ω = √(g/L)).\n- Change the rider’s mass.\n- Try longer chains at the same ω.',
    explain: 'At steady motion the chair moves in a circle of radius **r = d + L sin θ**. Vertically **T cos θ = mg**; horizontally **T sin θ = m ω² (d + L sin θ)**. So **tan θ = ω²(d + L sin θ)/g** — the mass cancels, so every chair on equal chains swings to the same angle whoever sits in it. The equation has no simple closed form; the lab solves it numerically. With d = 0 it becomes the conical pendulum, cos θ = g/(ω²L). When ω changes, the chairs swing to the new angle (a damped pendulum in the rotating frame; damping 0.35 s⁻¹ stands for air drag and joint friction).',
    apply: [q('cm12-a', 'What provides the centripetal force on a chair-o-plane rider?', ['the centrifugal force', 'the horizontal component of the chain tension', 'gravity', 'the seat belt'], 1, 'T sin θ.'), q('cm12-b', 'A heavier rider on the same chain swings out…', ['more', 'less', 'to the same angle', 'not at all'], 2, 'm cancels in tan θ = ω²(d + L sin θ)/g.'), q('cm12-c', 'With the arm length d = 0 the ride behaves like…', ['a simple pendulum', 'a conical pendulum', 'a car on a banked road', 'a satellite'], 1, 'Same equations.'), q('cm12-d', 'At θ = 45°, the chain tension is…', ['mg', '√2 mg', '2 mg', 'mg/√2'], 1, 'T = mg / cos θ.')],
